# Key Learning
HTML attachments are an effective phishing delivery mechanism because they do not contain a payload themselves — they simply redirect the browser to an attacker-controlled page, bypassing many attachment scanning controls. Open directory listings on attacker infrastructure can expose the entire phishing kit, credential logs, and even hidden flags, making enumeration of the base URL a valuable investigation step. Phishing kits frequently contain hardcoded attacker email addresses in PHP scripts, providing a persistent indicator of compromise that can be used for attribution and cross-campaign correlation. Base64-encoded strings stored on attacker servers may require additional operations such as reversing to reveal the final plaintext.

# What I Would Flag in a Real SOC
- HTML attachments in emails should be treated with the same scrutiny as executable attachments, browser-based redirection via HTML files is a deliberate bypass of gateway attachment scanning
- The sending domain groupmarketingonline.icu uses a non-standard TLD (.icu) which is frequently associated with phishing infrastructure and should be added to a blocklist upon identification
- Open directory listings on attacker-controlled infrastructure indicate poor operational security and should be documented as an opportunity to enumerate the full scope of the campaign
- A credential log exposed on the phishing server containing victim email addresses and IP addresses constitutes a live data breach and should be escalated immediately for victim notification
- Hardcoded Yandex email addresses in phishing kit PHP scripts are a known attacker pattern, m3npat@yandex.com should be submitted to threat intelligence platforms and cross-referenced against known campaigns
- The SHA256 hash of the phishing kit being flagged as trojan and hacktool on VirusTotal confirms this is a reused kit with prior detections, hash-based blocking should be applied across all endpoints in the environment
